# Target C — Section 141 coincident-apex repair and pure-tooth train consequence

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working repair candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This note repairs one missing case in normalized post-126 Section 141 and records a
finite consequence for the exact Section-126 pure-tooth endpoint.

Section 141 studies two consecutive Type-II tooth sides

\[
p_i p_{i+1},\qquad p_{i+1}p_{i+2}
\]

with nonunit base lengths

\[
b_i=1+e_i,\qquad b_{i+1}=1+e_{i+1},\qquad 0<e_i,e_{i+1}<1,
\]

and unit-tooth apexes `x_i,x_{i+1}`. It defines

\[
\beta(e):=\arccos{1+e\over2}\in(0,\pi/3)
\]

and proves, after fixing the ray order, that unit separation of the two apexes gives

\[
\alpha_i\le
\left({\pi\over3}-\beta(e_i)\right)
+
\left({\pi\over3}-\beta(e_{i+1})\right),
\tag{R141.1}
\]

where `alpha_i` is the exterior turn of the loaded force hull at `p_{i+1}`.

The written proof used `|x_i-x_{i+1}|>=1` without first excluding
`x_i=x_{i+1}`. The equality case is not formally covered by unit separation because it
is one source point, not two distinct source points. In the Type-II scope the missing
case is impossible for a separate geometric reason: near-disk convergence makes every
loaded-hull exterior turn uniformly `o(1)`, whereas a coincident pair of tooth apexes
would force a turn strictly larger than `pi/3`.

The repair has a useful exact corollary: in the Section-126 pure-tooth endpoint there
are no singleton stopped trains for all sufficiently large Type-II sources. Therefore
all `q` stopped trains are genuine strict-rhombus train tracks and

\[
\boxed{R_4\le {q^2\over4}.}
\tag{R141.2}
\]

Since canonical Type II has

\[
{R_4\over D^2}\to\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\]

this yields the endpoint-specific asymptotic floor

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q\over D}
\ge 2\sqrt{\Delta_2}
=2\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2\sqrt3}}
=1.9046256137279147\ldots .
}
\tag{R141.3}
\]

This does **not** revive the rejected global claim
`liminf (q-k)/D>=1.9046256...`. Equation (R141.3) is restricted to the exact
Section-126 pure-tooth one-cell endpoint, where `k=0` and every stopped train is shown
below to be a non-singleton rhombus train.

---

## 1. Type-II near-disk convergence makes the maximum hull turn vanish

Let

\[
F_D=\operatorname{conv}(B_D)
\]

be the loaded force hull. Canonical Section 80 gives centers `o_D` and errors
`delta_D=o(D)` such that

\[
d_H(F_D,B(o_D,D/2))\le\delta_D.
\tag{R141.4}
\]

The centralized Type-II loaded-hull-side theorem already uses the resulting finite
normal-cone estimate. Put

\[
r_D={D\over2}-\delta_D,
\qquad
R_{+,D}={D\over2}+\delta_D,
\qquad
\varepsilon_D:=\arccos{r_D\over R_{+,D}}.
\tag{R141.5}
\]

Then `epsilon_D->0`. For every boundary point `p` of `F_D` and every outward unit
supporting normal `n` at `p`, the same support-function argument gives

\[
\angle(n,p-o_D)\le\varepsilon_D.
\tag{R141.6}
\]

At a loaded hull vertex, take the outward normals of its two incident hull sides.
Their angular separation is exactly the exterior turning angle `alpha_i`. Both normals
lie within `epsilon_D` of the same radial direction `p-o_D`; hence

\[
\boxed{
0<\alpha_i\le2\varepsilon_D=o(1)
\quad\text{uniformly in }i.
}
\tag{R141.7}
\]

In particular, for all sufficiently large Type-II sources,

\[
\boxed{\max_i\alpha_i<\pi/3.}
\tag{R141.8}
\]

No absolute Hausdorff rate is needed.

---

## 2. Consecutive tooth apexes cannot coincide

Use the exact ray notation of normalized Section 141. Direct the preceding loaded-hull
side at argument zero. The ray from `p_{i+1}` to the first tooth apex has argument

\[
\theta_i=\pi-\beta(e_i),
\tag{R141.9}
\]

and the ray from `p_{i+1}` to the next tooth apex has argument

\[
\theta_{i+1}=\alpha_i+\beta(e_{i+1}).
\tag{R141.10}
\]

Suppose

\[
x_i=x_{i+1}.
\]

The two rays are then the same physical ray. In the inward angular lifts used in
(R141.9)--(R141.10), this gives

\[
\pi-\beta(e_i)
=\alpha_i+\beta(e_{i+1}),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
\alpha_i
=\pi-\beta(e_i)-\beta(e_{i+1})
>\pi/3,
}
\tag{R141.11}
\]

because both tooth bases are nonunit and therefore
`beta(e_i),beta(e_{i+1})<pi/3`.

Equation (R141.11) contradicts (R141.8). Thus for all sufficiently large Type-II
survivors,

\[
\boxed{x_i\ne x_{i+1}}
\tag{R141.12}
\]

whenever the two adjacent hull sides are two-edge nonunit teeth.

Now the two apexes are distinct source points, so source unit separation legitimately
gives

\[
|x_i-x_{i+1}|\ge1.
\]

The repaired ray-order argument in normalized Section 141 then applies exactly as
written and proves (R141.1). Thus the numerical conclusions (141.2)--(141.5) remain
valid in their stated sufficiently-large Type-II scope; the missing distinctness step
is supplied by (R141.7)--(R141.12).

---

## 3. Exact pure-tooth endpoint has no singleton stopped train

Assume now the exact Section-126 endpoint:

1. there is one unloaded positive-contact component;
2. its attachment set is the full loaded set `B`, `|B|=q`;
3. the augmented graph has
   \[
   T_\triangle=q,\qquad Q^+=0.
   \]

Section 126 proves

\[
e_{BB}=k=d_H=s_Z=Q=0,
\qquad h=q,
\qquad b=2q,
\tag{R141.13}
\]

and every bounded positive-contact face is a strict unit rhombus. It also proves that
the full contact graph has exactly `q` stopped trains, where an edge incident to no
rhombus is allowed a priori as a singleton bridge train.

The centralized Type-II loaded-hull theorem gives

\[
e_{BB}=q-m,
\]

where `m` is the number of nonunit loaded-hull sides. Since `e_BB=0`,

\[
\boxed{m=q.}
\tag{R141.14}
\]

Thus every loaded-hull side is nonunit. Normalized Section 130 identifies augmented
triangles exactly with two-edge nonunit outer teeth. Since `T_\triangle=q=m`, every
loaded-hull interval is therefore

\[
p_i-x_i-p_{i+1}
\tag{R141.15}
\]

with two unit contact edges and unloaded apex `x_i`.

Suppose a singleton stopped train existed. Its unique contact edge is incident to no
bounded rhombus. Because every bounded face is a rhombus, both sides of that edge are
the outer face, so it is a bridge and occurs twice in the occurrence-counted outer
walk.

But (R141.15) exhausts that outer walk. Every outer contact edge joins one loaded
vertex to one tooth apex. If a physical bridge edge incident to `p_i` occurs twice,
its two occurrences can only be the terminal legs of the two hull intervals adjacent
to `p_i`; a two-edge interval itself cannot traverse one edge twice because its loaded
endpoints are distinct. Hence

\[
x_{i-1}=x_i.
\]

This contradicts (R141.12). Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\text{the exact Section-126 Type-II endpoint has no singleton stopped train}
}
\tag{R141.16}
\]

for all sufficiently large `D`.

Consequently every one of the `q` stopped trains is a genuine train track of the full
strict-rhombus complex.

---

## 4. Train crossing gives the pure-endpoint `1.9046256...` floor

Apply canonical Theorem 10.6 to the collection of all bounded strict-rhombus faces.
With `T=q` genuine train tracks, its simple triangle-free train crossing graph gives

\[
R_4\le {T^2\over4}={q^2\over4},
\]

which is (R141.2).

Canonical Type II gives

\[
{R_4\over D^2}\to\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

Taking square roots proves (R141.3).

The logical scope is important. The recently rejected global `1.9046256...` loaded-hull
floor attempted to derive the same constant before establishing the exact pure-tooth
normal form and was algebraically invalid. Here the constant follows from the physical
rhombus crossing count after singleton trains have been excluded. It therefore says
nothing about a general Type-II survivor with augmented defect, multiple cells, deep
outer routes, exceptional faces, or `k>0`.

---

## 5. What this changes and what remains open

The repair removes two ambiguities from the post-126 endpoint.

1. Normalized Section 141's use of apex unit separation is now justified in its
   sufficiently-large Type-II scope.
2. The exact pure-tooth Section-126 endpoint is not a rhombic system with an arbitrary
   linear population of bridge trains. Every stopped train traverses the strict-rhombus
   complex, and its train count must satisfy the finite Mantel bound (R141.2).

This still does not close the pure-tooth endpoint. The valid asymptotic lower bound
`q/D>=1.9046256...` is compatible with the near-disk perimeter bound
`q<per(F)=(pi+o(1))D`. The remaining exact obstruction is the Section-126
train-shear/diameter-thrackle matrix, now without singleton bridge columns, together
with the complete-grid diameter restrictions on the same `q` genuine trains.

**Disposition:** repair candidate plus exact pure-endpoint sharpening. C-II and Level I
remain open.
